# Day 27 - Power of Four

## Problem Statement
Given an integer `n`, return `true` if it is a power of four. Otherwise, return `false`.

An integer `n` is a power of four if there exists an integer `x` such that `n = 4^x`.

## Code

class Solution {
public:
    bool isPowerOfFour(int n) {
        if (n == 1) {
            return true;
        }
        else if (n <= 0 || n % 4 != 0) {
            return false;
        }

        return isPowerOfFour(n / 4);
    }
};



## Time Complexity
**O(log₄ n)**

## Space Complexity
**O(log₄ n)**

## Approach
This solution uses **recursion** to repeatedly divide the given number by `4`. If the number becomes `1`, it means the original number can be expressed as a power of `4`, so the function returns `true`. If the number is non-positive or is not divisible by `4`, it cannot be a power of `4`, and the function returns `false`. Since the number is divided by `4` in every recursive call, the recursion depth is **log₄ n**, resulting in **O(log₄ n)** time and space complexity.
